LUX.studies({"life":{"lede":"The life of Jesus told in order, from the angel’s message in Nazareth to the empty tomb and Pentecost, each scene linked to the Gospels that tell it.","intro":["This walk follows Jesus of Nazareth through his life as a story, in seven acts and some sixty scenes. Each scene names the Gospels that tell it, gives a line or two of their own words, and points to other witnesses in the archive, from Josephus and Tacitus to inscriptions in stone, where they bear on the event.","The anchors are few and firm. Jesus was born c. 6–4 BC, before the death of Herod the Great; his ministry began c. AD 28–29, in the fifteenth year of Tiberius; and he was crucified under Pontius Pilate, c. AD 30 or 33. Within those bounds the Gospels sometimes order episodes differently, and the sequence here is one reasonable path the reader can weigh against the texts."],"acts":[{"id":"the-coming","title":"The Coming","span":"c. 6–4 BC","art":"nativity-botticelli","lede":"An angel’s message in Nazareth, a birth in Bethlehem in the last years of Herod the Great, and a family driven into exile.","events":[{"id":"annunciation","title":"The annunciation to Mary","when":"c. 7–5 BC","place":"nazareth","summary":"In Nazareth, a village in Galilee, the angel Gabriel comes to Mary, a young woman betrothed to Joseph. He tells her she will conceive by the Holy Spirit and bear a son to be named Jesus, heir to the throne of David. Mary answers that she is the Lord’s servant.","refs":[{"label":"Luke 1:26–38","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-1"}],"quote":{"ref":"Luke 1:31","text":"Behold, you will conceive in your womb and give birth to a son, and shall name him ‘Jesus.’"},"witness":["scripture/quran/surah-19"]},{"id":"josephs-dream","title":"Joseph’s dream","when":"c. 7–5 BC","place":null,"summary":"Joseph learns that Mary is pregnant and resolves to end the betrothal quietly. An angel tells him in a dream to take her as his wife and to name the child Jesus. Matthew links the birth to Isaiah’s sign of a child called Emmanuel, “God with us.”","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 1:18–25","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-1"}],"quote":null,"witness":[]},{"id":"birth-in-bethlehem","title":"The birth in Bethlehem","when":"c. 6–4 BC","place":"bethlehem","summary":"Luke tells of a census that brings Joseph and Mary from Nazareth to Bethlehem, the town of David, where Mary gives birth and lays the child in a feeding trough because there is no room in the inn. Matthew too places the birth in Bethlehem in the days of King Herod. Neither Gospel gives a day or month; December 25 is a later liturgical date.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 2:1","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-2"},{"label":"Luke 2:1–7","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-2"}],"quote":null,"witness":["gospels/infancy-gospel-of-james"]},{"id":"shepherds","title":"The shepherds and the angels","when":"c. 6–4 BC","place":"bethlehem","summary":"Shepherds keeping watch by night near Bethlehem see an angel and hear a multitude of the heavenly host praising God. They hurry to find the child in the manger and spread the news of what they were told.","refs":[{"label":"Luke 2:8–20","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-2"}],"quote":{"ref":"Luke 2:11","text":"For there is born to you today, in David’s city, a Savior, who is Christ the Lord."},"witness":[]},{"id":"presentation","title":"The presentation in the Temple","when":"c. 6–4 BC","place":"jerusalem","summary":"On the eighth day the child is circumcised and named Jesus. His parents later bring him to the Temple in Jerusalem, where the aged Simeon takes him in his arms and praises God, and the prophetess Anna speaks of him to all awaiting redemption.","refs":[{"label":"Luke 2:21–38","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-2"}],"quote":null,"witness":[]},{"id":"magi","title":"The wise men from the east","when":"c. 6–4 BC","place":"bethlehem","summary":"Wise men, or magi, follow a star to Jerusalem asking for the newborn king of the Jews, alarming Herod the Great, the Roman-backed king of Judea. Directed to Bethlehem, they find the child with Mary and offer gold, frankincense and myrrh. Matthew does not call them kings, number them or name them.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 2:1–12","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-2"}],"quote":{"ref":"Matthew 2:2","text":"Where is he who is born King of the Jews? For we saw his star in the east, and have come to worship him."},"witness":[]},{"id":"flight-into-egypt","title":"Flight into Egypt and return to Nazareth","when":"c. 6–4 BC","place":"nazareth","summary":"Warned in a dream, Joseph takes the child and his mother to Egypt, and Herod orders the killing of Bethlehem’s boys aged two and under. After Herod’s death the family returns and, avoiding his son Archelaus in Judea, settles in Nazareth. Luke, for his part, simply has the family go home to Nazareth after the Temple visit.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 2:13–23","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-2"},{"label":"Luke 2:39–40","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-2"}],"quote":null,"witness":[]}]},{"id":"nazareth-and-the-jordan","title":"Nazareth and the Jordan","span":"c. AD 6–29","art":"baptism","lede":"Long quiet years in a Galilean village, then a prophet at the Jordan, a baptism, a wilderness testing and the first followers.","events":[{"id":"boy-in-the-temple","title":"The boy in the Temple","when":"c. AD 6–8","place":"jerusalem","summary":"At twelve, Jesus goes up to Jerusalem with his parents for Passover and stays behind. After three days they find him in the Temple, listening to the teachers and asking questions. He returns with them to Nazareth and grows in wisdom and stature.","refs":[{"label":"Luke 2:41–52","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-2"}],"quote":{"ref":"Luke 2:49","text":"Why were you looking for me? Didn’t you know that I must be in my Father’s house?"},"witness":[]},{"id":"hidden-years","title":"The carpenter of Nazareth","when":"c. AD 8–28","place":"nazareth","summary":"Of the next eighteen years or so the Gospels say almost nothing. Later, his neighbours in Nazareth remember him as the carpenter, or builder, the son of Mary, with brothers named James, Joses, Judas and Simon, and sisters. The family is a household of Galilean Jews, and Aramaic was probably his everyday language.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 13:53–58","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-13"},{"label":"Mark 6:1–6","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-6"}],"quote":null,"witness":["witnesses/josephus-antiquities-20"]},{"id":"john-the-baptist","title":"John the Baptist in the wilderness","when":"c. AD 28–29","place":"jordan-river","summary":"In the fifteenth year of the emperor Tiberius, John, son of the priest Zechariah, appears in the wilderness by the Jordan calling Israel to repentance and baptizing. Crowds come out to him, and he announces one coming after him who is mightier. Josephus, the first-century Jewish historian, also remembers John as a good man who baptized and was put to death by Herod Antipas.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 3:1–12","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-3"},{"label":"Mark 1:1–8","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-1"},{"label":"Luke 3:1–18","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-3"},{"label":"John 1:19–28","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-1"}],"quote":null,"witness":["witnesses/josephus-antiquities-18"]},{"id":"baptism","title":"The baptism of Jesus","when":"c. AD 28–29","place":"jordan-river","summary":"Jesus comes from Nazareth and is baptized by John in the Jordan. As he comes up from the water the Spirit descends on him like a dove, and a voice from heaven names him the beloved Son. Luke notes that he was about thirty years old.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 3:13–17","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-3"},{"label":"Mark 1:9–11","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-1"},{"label":"Luke 3:21–23","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-3"},{"label":"John 1:29–34","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-1"}],"quote":{"ref":"Mark 1:11","text":"You are my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased."},"witness":[]},{"id":"temptation","title":"Forty days in the wilderness","when":"c. AD 28–29","place":null,"summary":"The Spirit drives Jesus into the wilderness, where he fasts forty days and is tested by Satan. In Matthew and Luke he answers each temptation, to turn stones into bread, to test God and to grasp at the kingdoms of the world, with words of Scripture.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 4:1–11","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-4"},{"label":"Mark 1:12–13","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-1"},{"label":"Luke 4:1–13","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-4"}],"quote":null,"witness":[]},{"id":"first-disciples","title":"The Lamb of God and the first disciples","when":"c. AD 28–29","place":null,"summary":"In John’s Gospel the Baptist points Jesus out as the Lamb of God, and two of John’s own disciples follow him. One, Andrew, brings his brother Simon, whom Jesus names Cephas, “rock”; Philip brings Nathanael, who doubts that anything good can come from Nazareth.","refs":[{"label":"John 1:35–51","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-1"}],"quote":{"ref":"John 1:46","text":"Nathanael said to him, “Can any good thing come out of Nazareth?” Philip said to him, “Come and see.”"},"witness":[]},{"id":"cana","title":"Water into wine at Cana","when":"c. AD 28–29","place":null,"summary":"At a wedding in Cana of Galilee the wine runs out, and Jesus’s mother tells the servants to do whatever he says. He has six stone water jars filled, and the steward tastes wine better than what came before. John calls it the first of his signs.","refs":[{"label":"John 2:1–11","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-2"}],"quote":null,"witness":[]}]},{"id":"kingdom-in-galilee","title":"The Kingdom in Galilee","span":"c. AD 28–30","art":"calling-matthew","lede":"After John’s arrest Jesus announces the kingdom of God in the towns around the Sea of Galilee, calls fishermen and a tax collector, and teaches on a mountain.","events":[{"id":"nazareth-synagogue","title":"The sermon at Nazareth","when":"c. AD 28–30","place":"nazareth","summary":"Back in his home synagogue, Jesus reads from the scroll of Isaiah: the Spirit of the Lord has anointed him to bring good news to the poor and freedom to captives. He declares the words fulfilled that day, and admiration turns to fury when he recalls the prophets Elijah and Elisha helping foreigners. Mark and Matthew place a hometown rejection later in the ministry.","refs":[{"label":"Luke 4:16–30","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-4"}],"quote":{"ref":"Luke 4:21","text":"Today, this Scripture has been fulfilled in your hearing."},"witness":["archaeology/dead-sea-scrolls-isaiah"]},{"id":"calling-fishermen","title":"Fishers of men","when":"c. AD 28–30","place":"sea-of-galilee","summary":"Walking by the Sea of Galilee, Jesus calls two pairs of brothers at their nets, Simon and Andrew, James and John, the sons of Zebedee. They leave their boats and follow him. Luke sets Simon’s call after a night of empty nets and a sudden great catch.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 4:18–22","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-4"},{"label":"Mark 1:16–20","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-1"},{"label":"Luke 5:1–11","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-5"}],"quote":{"ref":"Mark 1:17","text":"Come after me, and I will make you into fishers for men."},"witness":[]},{"id":"capernaum-sabbath","title":"A Sabbath in Capernaum","when":"c. AD 28–30","place":"capernaum","summary":"In the synagogue at Capernaum, the lakeside town that becomes his base, Jesus teaches with authority and drives out an unclean spirit. He goes to Simon’s house and lifts Simon’s mother-in-law from her fever, and at sunset the whole town gathers at the door with its sick.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 8:14–17","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-8"},{"label":"Mark 1:21–34","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-1"},{"label":"Luke 4:31–41","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-4"}],"quote":null,"witness":[]},{"id":"paralytic","title":"The paralysed man let down through the roof","when":"c. AD 28–30","place":"capernaum","summary":"So many crowd the house that four friends dig through the roof and lower a paralysed man to Jesus. He tells the man his sins are forgiven, and when scribes object that only God can forgive, he tells him to rise, take his mat and walk home.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 9:1–8","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-9"},{"label":"Mark 2:1–12","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-2"},{"label":"Luke 5:17–26","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-5"}],"quote":null,"witness":[]},{"id":"levi","title":"The call of Levi","when":"c. AD 28–30","place":"capernaum","summary":"Jesus calls a tax collector from his booth, named Matthew in the first Gospel and Levi in Mark and Luke, and he gets up and follows. At the meal that follows Jesus eats with tax collectors and sinners, and answers his critics that the sick, not the healthy, need a physician.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 9:9–13","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-9"},{"label":"Mark 2:13–17","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-2"},{"label":"Luke 5:27–32","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-5"}],"quote":{"ref":"Mark 2:17","text":"Those who are healthy have no need for a physician, but those who are sick."},"witness":[]},{"id":"the-twelve","title":"The choosing of the Twelve","when":"c. AD 28–30","place":null,"summary":"After a night of prayer on a mountain, Jesus chooses twelve from among his followers to be with him and to be sent out to preach. The lists name Simon Peter, Andrew, James, John, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas and others, ending with Judas Iscariot, who would betray him. Paul already speaks of “the Twelve” in the 50s.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 10:1–4","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-10"},{"label":"Mark 3:13–19","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-3"},{"label":"Luke 6:12–16","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-6"}],"quote":null,"witness":["scripture/new-testament/07-1-corinthians"]},{"id":"sermon-on-the-mount","title":"The Sermon on the Mount","when":"c. AD 28–30","place":"sea-of-galilee","summary":"Seated on a hillside, Jesus teaches the blessings on the poor in spirit and the merciful, love of enemies, prayer to the Father in secret, and trust instead of anxiety, ending with the house built on rock. Luke gives a shorter related sermon on a level place.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 5:1–7:29","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-5"},{"label":"Luke 6:17–49","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-6"}],"quote":{"ref":"Matthew 5:3","text":"Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the Kingdom of Heaven."},"witness":[]}]},{"id":"signs-and-parables","title":"Parables and Signs","span":"c. AD 29–32","art":"transfiguration","lede":"Stories of seed and harvest, a storm stilled, a girl raised, a crowd fed, and on a mountain a disciple’s confession and a vision of glory.","events":[{"id":"sower","title":"The parable of the sower","when":"Galilean ministry","place":"sea-of-galilee","summary":"Teaching from a boat to a crowd on the shore, Jesus tells of seed falling on the path, on rock, among thorns and on good soil. Privately he explains it as the different ways people receive the word. It opens a chain of parables about the kingdom: the mustard seed, the leaven, the hidden treasure.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 13:1–23","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-13"},{"label":"Mark 4:1–20","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-4"},{"label":"Luke 8:4–15","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-8"}],"quote":null,"witness":[]},{"id":"calming-storm","title":"The storm on the lake","when":"Galilean ministry","place":"sea-of-galilee","summary":"Crossing the lake at evening, the boat is swamped by a violent squall while Jesus sleeps on a cushion. The disciples wake him; he rebukes the wind and the sea becomes calm, and they ask one another who this is.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 8:23–27","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-8"},{"label":"Mark 4:35–41","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-4"},{"label":"Luke 8:22–25","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-8"}],"quote":{"ref":"Mark 4:41","text":"Who then is this, that even the wind and the sea obey him?"},"witness":[]},{"id":"jairus","title":"Jairus’s daughter and the woman who touched his cloak","when":"Galilean ministry","place":null,"summary":"Jairus, a synagogue leader, begs Jesus to come to his dying daughter. On the way a woman who has bled for twelve years touches his cloak and is healed. Word comes that the girl has died, but Jesus takes her by the hand and she rises.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 9:18–26","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-9"},{"label":"Mark 5:21–43","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-5"},{"label":"Luke 8:40–56","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-8"}],"quote":{"ref":"Mark 5:41","text":"Taking the child by the hand, he said to her, “Talitha cumi!” which means, being interpreted, “Girl, I tell you, get up!”"},"witness":[]},{"id":"death-of-john","title":"The death of John the Baptist","when":"c. AD 29–32","place":"machaerus","summary":"John has rebuked Herod Antipas, ruler of Galilee, for marrying his brother’s wife Herodias, and is imprisoned. At a birthday banquet Herodias’s daughter dances, and at her mother’s prompting asks for John’s head. Josephus places John’s imprisonment and death at the fortress of Machaerus and gives Herod’s fear of his influence as the motive.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 14:1–12","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-14"},{"label":"Mark 6:14–29","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-6"},{"label":"Luke 9:7–9","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-9"}],"quote":null,"witness":["witnesses/josephus-antiquities-18"]},{"id":"feeding-5000","title":"The feeding of the five thousand","when":"Near Passover (John 6:4)","place":"sea-of-galilee","summary":"A great crowd follows Jesus to a remote place by the lake. With five loaves and two fish he gives thanks, breaks the bread and has the disciples distribute it; all eat their fill and twelve baskets of fragments remain. It is the one miracle told in all four Gospels.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 14:13–21","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-14"},{"label":"Mark 6:30–44","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-6"},{"label":"Luke 9:10–17","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-9"},{"label":"John 6:1–15","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-6"}],"quote":null,"witness":[]},{"id":"walking-on-water","title":"Walking on the water","when":"Galilean ministry","place":"sea-of-galilee","summary":"That night the disciples row against the wind, and Jesus comes to them walking on the sea. They cry out in fear, thinking him a ghost. In Matthew, Peter steps out to meet him, begins to sink and is caught by the hand.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 14:22–33","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-14"},{"label":"Mark 6:45–52","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-6"},{"label":"John 6:16–21","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-6"}],"quote":null,"witness":[]},{"id":"peters-confession","title":"Peter’s confession","when":"Galilean ministry","place":null,"summary":"Near Caesarea Philippi, in the far north, Jesus asks who people say he is, and then who the disciples say he is. Peter answers that he is the Christ, the Messiah. Jesus then begins to teach that the Son of Man must suffer, be killed and rise again.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 16:13–23","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-16"},{"label":"Mark 8:27–33","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-8"},{"label":"Luke 9:18–22","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-9"}],"quote":{"ref":"Matthew 16:16","text":"Simon Peter answered, “You are the Christ, the Son of the living God.”"},"witness":[]},{"id":"transfiguration","title":"The transfiguration","when":"Galilean ministry","place":null,"summary":"Jesus takes Peter, James and John up a high mountain, and there he is changed before them, his clothes shining white. Moses and Elijah appear talking with him, a cloud overshadows them, and a voice speaks from it.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 17:1–13","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-17"},{"label":"Mark 9:2–13","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-9"},{"label":"Luke 9:28–36","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-9"}],"quote":{"ref":"Mark 9:7","text":"This is my beloved Son. Listen to him."},"witness":[]}]},{"id":"road-to-jerusalem","title":"The Road to Jerusalem","span":"c. AD 30–33","art":"tribute-money","lede":"Jesus sets his face toward Jerusalem, teaching in parables along the way, and at Bethany calls a dead friend from the tomb.","events":[{"id":"good-samaritan","title":"The good Samaritan","when":"On the journey","place":null,"summary":"Luke has Jesus turn resolutely toward Jerusalem and teach as he travels. When a lawyer asks who counts as his neighbour, Jesus tells of a man beaten by robbers, passed by a priest and a Levite, and rescued by a Samaritan, a member of a people many Jews regarded as outsiders.","refs":[{"label":"Luke 10:25–37","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-10"}],"quote":{"ref":"Luke 10:37","text":"He said, “He who showed mercy on him.” Then Jesus said to him, “Go and do likewise.”"},"witness":[]},{"id":"martha-and-mary","title":"Martha and Mary","when":"On the journey","place":"bethany","summary":"Jesus is welcomed by two sisters. Martha is busy with serving while Mary sits at his feet listening, and when Martha complains, Jesus says Mary has chosen the good part. Luke does not name the village; John places sisters of these names at Bethany, near Jerusalem.","refs":[{"label":"Luke 10:38–42","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-10"}],"quote":null,"witness":[]},{"id":"lost-and-found","title":"The lost sheep, the lost coin and the two sons","when":"On the journey","place":null,"summary":"Criticised for eating with sinners, Jesus tells three stories of things lost and found: a sheep, a coin, and a younger son who squanders his inheritance and comes home to a father who runs to embrace him. The elder brother’s resentment closes the story with a question left open.","refs":[{"label":"Luke 15:1–32","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-15"}],"quote":{"ref":"Luke 15:24","text":"for this, my son, was dead and is alive again. He was lost and is found."},"witness":[]},{"id":"man-born-blind","title":"The man born blind","when":"Festival of Booths or after","place":"jerusalem","summary":"In Jerusalem Jesus makes mud, spreads it on the eyes of a man blind from birth, and sends him to wash in the pool of Siloam. The man comes back seeing, and under questioning by the Pharisees holds to what he knows.","refs":[{"label":"John 9:1–41","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-9"}],"quote":null,"witness":[]},{"id":"lazarus","title":"The raising of Lazarus","when":"Before the last Passover","place":"bethany","summary":"Word comes that Lazarus of Bethany, brother of Martha and Mary, is ill. Jesus arrives to find him four days in the tomb; he weeps, has the stone rolled away and calls Lazarus out, and the dead man comes out still bound in grave cloths. John makes this the last and greatest of the signs.","refs":[{"label":"John 11:1–44","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-11"}],"quote":{"ref":"John 11:25","text":"I am the resurrection and the life. He who believes in me will still live, even if he dies."},"witness":[]},{"id":"council","title":"The council’s decision","when":"Before the last Passover","place":"jerusalem","summary":"Alarmed at the following Jesus is gathering, the chief priests and Pharisees meet in council. Caiaphas, high priest that year, argues that it is better for one man to die for the people than for the whole nation to perish. From that day they plan his death, and Jesus withdraws to a town called Ephraim.","refs":[{"label":"John 11:45–57","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-11"}],"quote":{"ref":"John 11:50","text":"it is advantageous for us that one man should die for the people, and that the whole nation not perish."},"witness":["archaeology/caiaphas-ossuary"]}]},{"id":"the-final-week","title":"The Final Week","span":"Passover, c. AD 30 or 33","art":"last-supper","lede":"A royal entry, a disturbance in the Temple, a last meal with his friends, betrayal by night, trials before priests and the Roman governor, and a cross outside the city.","events":[{"id":"anointing-at-bethany","title":"The anointing at Bethany","when":"Passover, c. AD 30 or 33","place":"bethany","summary":"At a dinner in Bethany a woman breaks a jar of costly ointment and pours it over Jesus. Some protest at the waste; Jesus says she has prepared his body for burial. John names her as Mary, sister of Lazarus, and has Judas object.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 26:6–13","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-26"},{"label":"Mark 14:3–9","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-14"},{"label":"John 12:1–8","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-12"}],"quote":null,"witness":[]},{"id":"triumphal-entry","title":"The entry into Jerusalem","when":"Passover, c. AD 30 or 33","place":"jerusalem","summary":"Jesus rides into Jerusalem on a young donkey as the crowds spread cloaks and branches on the road and shout Hosanna. Matthew and John see in it the prophet Zechariah’s picture of a humble king coming to Zion.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 21:1–11","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-21"},{"label":"Mark 11:1–11","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-11"},{"label":"Luke 19:28–40","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-19"},{"label":"John 12:12–19","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-12"}],"quote":{"ref":"Mark 11:9","text":"Hosanna! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!"},"witness":[]},{"id":"temple-cleansing","title":"The cleansing of the Temple","when":"Passover, c. AD 30 or 33","place":"jerusalem","summary":"In the Temple courts Jesus drives out those buying and selling and overturns the money changers’ tables, quoting Isaiah and Jeremiah. The chief priests and scribes begin seeking a way to destroy him. John tells a similar scene at the start of the ministry.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 21:12–17","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-21"},{"label":"Mark 11:15–19","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-11"},{"label":"Luke 19:45–48","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-19"},{"label":"John 2:13–22","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-2"}],"quote":null,"witness":[]},{"id":"last-supper","title":"The Last Supper","when":"Passover, c. AD 30 or 33","place":"jerusalem","summary":"On his last night Jesus shares a meal with the Twelve. He takes bread, gives thanks, breaks it and calls it his body, and takes the cup as the new covenant in his blood; he also foretells that one of them will betray him. Paul hands on the same words to the Corinthians in the 50s.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 26:17–30","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-26"},{"label":"Mark 14:12–26","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-14"},{"label":"Luke 22:7–23","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-22"}],"quote":{"ref":"Luke 22:19","text":"This is my body which is given for you. Do this in memory of me."},"witness":["scripture/new-testament/07-1-corinthians"]},{"id":"foot-washing","title":"The washing of the disciples’ feet","when":"Passover, c. AD 30 or 33","place":"jerusalem","summary":"In John’s account of the meal, Jesus rises from the table, ties a towel around himself and washes his disciples’ feet, overruling Peter’s protest. He gives them a new commandment, to love one another as he has loved them, and speaks at length of the Spirit to come.","refs":[{"label":"John 13:1–35","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-13"}],"quote":null,"witness":[]},{"id":"gethsemane","title":"Gethsemane","when":"Passover, c. AD 30 or 33","place":"jerusalem","summary":"On the Mount of Olives, in a place called Gethsemane, Jesus prays in anguish that the cup might pass from him, while Peter, James and John fall asleep. He yields himself to the Father’s will.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 26:36–46","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-26"},{"label":"Mark 14:32–42","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-14"},{"label":"Luke 22:39–46","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-22"},{"label":"John 18:1","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-18"}],"quote":{"ref":"Mark 14:36","text":"Abba, Father, all things are possible to you. Please remove this cup from me. However, not what I desire, but what you desire."},"witness":[]},{"id":"arrest","title":"The arrest","when":"Passover, c. AD 30 or 33","place":"jerusalem","summary":"Judas Iscariot arrives with an armed crowd from the chief priests and identifies Jesus with a kiss. A disciple, named in John as Peter, cuts off the ear of the high priest’s servant, Malchus. The disciples flee.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 26:47–56","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-26"},{"label":"Mark 14:43–52","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-14"},{"label":"Luke 22:47–53","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-22"},{"label":"John 18:2–12","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-18"}],"quote":null,"witness":[]},{"id":"annas-and-caiaphas","title":"Before Annas and Caiaphas","when":"Passover, c. AD 30 or 33","place":"jerusalem","summary":"Jesus is taken first, in John, to Annas, the former high priest, and then to his son-in-law Caiaphas, the serving high priest. Before the council witnesses disagree, until the high priest asks him directly whether he is the Christ. His answer is condemned as blasphemy.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 26:57–68","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-26"},{"label":"Mark 14:53–65","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-14"},{"label":"Luke 22:54–71","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-22"},{"label":"John 18:12–24","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-18"}],"quote":null,"witness":["archaeology/caiaphas-ossuary","witnesses/josephus-antiquities-18"]},{"id":"peters-denial","title":"Peter’s denial","when":"Passover, c. AD 30 or 33","place":"jerusalem","summary":"Warming himself in the high priest’s courtyard, Peter is recognised as one of Jesus’s followers and three times denies knowing him. The rooster crows, Peter remembers what Jesus had said, and weeps.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 26:69–75","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-26"},{"label":"Mark 14:66–72","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-14"},{"label":"Luke 22:54–62","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-22"},{"label":"John 18:15–27","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-18"}],"quote":null,"witness":[]},{"id":"before-pilate","title":"Before Pontius Pilate","when":"Passover, c. AD 30 or 33","place":"jerusalem","summary":"At daybreak Jesus is handed over to Pontius Pilate, the Roman prefect of Judea from AD 26 to 36. Pilate asks whether he is the king of the Jews; in John, Jesus answers that he has come to testify to the truth. A stone found at Caesarea Maritima in 1961 bears Pilate’s name and title.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 27:1–14","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-27"},{"label":"Mark 15:1–5","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-15"},{"label":"Luke 23:1–5","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-23"},{"label":"John 18:28–38","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-18"}],"quote":{"ref":"John 18:38","text":"Pilate said to him, “What is truth?”"},"witness":["archaeology/pilate-inscription","witnesses/tacitus-annals-15-44","witnesses/josephus-antiquities-18"]},{"id":"before-herod","title":"Before Herod Antipas","when":"Passover, c. AD 30 or 33","place":"jerusalem","summary":"Learning that Jesus is a Galilean, Pilate sends him to Herod Antipas, ruler of Galilee, who is in Jerusalem for the feast. Herod hopes to see a miracle, but Jesus gives him no answer, and Herod’s soldiers mock him and send him back in a splendid robe.","refs":[{"label":"Luke 23:6–12","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-23"}],"quote":null,"witness":[]},{"id":"sentence","title":"Barabbas, the scourging and the sentence","when":"Passover, c. AD 30 or 33","place":"jerusalem","summary":"Pilate offers to release a prisoner for the feast, and the crowd chooses Barabbas. Jesus is flogged, crowned with thorns and mocked by the soldiers as king, and Pilate hands him over to be crucified.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 27:15–31","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-27"},{"label":"Mark 15:6–20","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-15"},{"label":"Luke 23:13–25","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-23"},{"label":"John 18:39–19:16","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-18"}],"quote":{"ref":"John 19:5","text":"Jesus therefore came out, wearing the crown of thorns and the purple garment. Pilate said to them, “Behold, the man!”"},"witness":[]},{"id":"crucifixion","title":"The crucifixion","when":"Passover, c. AD 30 or 33","place":"jerusalem","summary":"Simon of Cyrene is made to carry the cross to Golgotha, “the place of a skull,” outside the city, where Jesus is crucified between two others under the charge “King of the Jews.” Soldiers divide his clothes, passers-by mock him, and in Luke one of the men crucified with him asks to be remembered. The Roman historian Tacitus records that Christ was executed under Tiberius by the procurator Pontius Pilate.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 27:32–44","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-27"},{"label":"Mark 15:21–32","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-15"},{"label":"Luke 23:26–43","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-23"},{"label":"John 19:17–27","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-19"}],"quote":{"ref":"Luke 23:42–43","text":"Lord, remember me when you come into your Kingdom.” Jesus said to him, “Assuredly I tell you, today you will be with me in Paradise."},"witness":["witnesses/tacitus-annals-15-44","witnesses/josephus-antiquities-18","witnesses/mara-bar-serapion","witnesses/talmud-sanhedrin-43a","witnesses/lucan-peregrinus"]},{"id":"death","title":"The death of Jesus","when":"Passover, c. AD 30 or 33","place":"jerusalem","summary":"Darkness covers the land from noon until three in the afternoon. Jesus cries out the opening words of Psalm 22 and breathes his last; the Temple curtain is torn in two, and the centurion standing opposite confesses him. In John his last word is “It is finished.”","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 27:45–56","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-27"},{"label":"Mark 15:33–41","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-15"},{"label":"Luke 23:44–49","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-23"},{"label":"John 19:28–37","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-19"}],"quote":{"ref":"Mark 15:39","text":"Truly this man was the Son of God!"},"witness":["witnesses/thallus-phlegon-fragments"]},{"id":"burial","title":"The burial","when":"Passover, c. AD 30 or 33","place":"jerusalem","summary":"Joseph of Arimathea, a respected council member, asks Pilate for the body. He wraps it in linen and lays it in a tomb cut from rock, and a stone is rolled across the entrance; John adds that Nicodemus brought spices. Mary Magdalene and other women watch where he is laid.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 27:57–66","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-27"},{"label":"Mark 15:42–47","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-15"},{"label":"Luke 23:50–56","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-23"},{"label":"John 19:38–42","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-19"}],"quote":null,"witness":["scripture/new-testament/07-1-corinthians","gospels/gospel-of-peter"]}]},{"id":"the-risen-lord","title":"The Risen Lord","span":"From the third day","art":"resurrection","lede":"An empty tomb at dawn, then meetings with the risen Jesus in a garden, on a road, in a locked room and by the lake, until his ascension and the gift of the Spirit.","events":[{"id":"empty-tomb","title":"The women at the empty tomb","when":"Sunday dawn","place":"jerusalem","summary":"Early on the first day of the week Mary Magdalene and other women come to the tomb with spices and find the stone rolled away. A young man in white, or angels, tell them Jesus has risen. Mary Magdalene is named in every account; in John, Peter and another disciple run to see the empty grave cloths.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 28:1–8","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-28"},{"label":"Mark 16:1–8","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-16"},{"label":"Luke 24:1–12","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-24"},{"label":"John 20:1–10","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-20"}],"quote":{"ref":"Mark 16:6","text":"You seek Jesus, the Nazarene, who has been crucified. He has risen! He is not here."},"witness":["scripture/new-testament/07-1-corinthians","gospels/gospel-of-peter"]},{"id":"mary-magdalene","title":"Mary Magdalene in the garden","when":"Sunday","place":"jerusalem","summary":"Weeping outside the tomb, Mary Magdalene sees a man she takes for the gardener. He speaks her name, she recognises Jesus, and he sends her to tell the disciples. Matthew has Jesus meet the women as they hurry from the tomb.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 28:9–10","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-28"},{"label":"John 20:11–18","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-20"}],"quote":{"ref":"John 20:16","text":"Jesus said to her, “Mary.” She turned and said to him, “Rabboni!” which is to say, “Teacher!”"},"witness":[]},{"id":"emmaus","title":"The road to Emmaus","when":"Sunday","place":"emmaus","summary":"Two disciples, one named Cleopas, are walking to the village of Emmaus when a stranger joins them and explains the Scriptures. At supper he breaks the bread and they recognise Jesus, who vanishes. Hurrying back to Jerusalem, they hear that the Lord has appeared to Simon.","refs":[{"label":"Luke 24:13–35","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-24"}],"quote":{"ref":"Luke 24:32","text":"Weren’t our hearts burning within us while he spoke to us along the way, and while he opened the Scriptures to us?"},"witness":[]},{"id":"the-eleven","title":"Among the disciples in Jerusalem","when":"Sunday evening","place":"jerusalem","summary":"That evening Jesus stands among the disciples behind closed doors and greets them with peace. In Luke he shows his hands and feet and eats fish before them; in John he breathes on them and says, “Receive the Holy Spirit.”","refs":[{"label":"Luke 24:36–49","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-24"},{"label":"John 20:19–23","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-20"}],"quote":null,"witness":[]},{"id":"thomas","title":"Thomas","when":"A week later","place":"jerusalem","summary":"Thomas, one of the Twelve, was absent and refuses to believe without seeing the wounds. Eight days later Jesus appears again and invites him to look and touch, and Thomas answers with the Gospel’s fullest confession.","refs":[{"label":"John 20:24–29","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-20"}],"quote":{"ref":"John 20:28–29","text":"Thomas answered him, “My Lord and my God!” Jesus said to him, “Because you have seen me, you have believed. Blessed are those who have not seen and have believed.”"},"witness":[]},{"id":"lakeside","title":"Breakfast by the lake","when":"The third day and after","place":"sea-of-galilee","summary":"Back in Galilee, seven disciples fish all night and catch nothing. At dawn a figure on the shore tells them where to cast the net, and it fills with 153 fish; Jesus has a fire ready with bread and fish. Three times he asks Peter whether he loves him, and three times charges him to feed his sheep.","refs":[{"label":"John 21:1–23","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-21"}],"quote":null,"witness":[]},{"id":"great-commission","title":"The commission on the mountain","when":"The third day and after","place":null,"summary":"The eleven disciples go to a mountain in Galilee as Jesus had told them. Some worship and some doubt. He sends them to make disciples of all nations, baptizing and teaching, and promises to be with them always.","refs":[{"label":"Matthew 28:16–20","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-28"}],"quote":{"ref":"Matthew 28:19–20","text":"Go and make disciples of all nations … Behold, I am with you always, even to the end of the age."},"witness":[]},{"id":"five-hundred-and-james","title":"Cephas, the five hundred and James","when":"The third day and after","place":null,"summary":"Writing to Corinth around AD 54, Paul hands on a list he had himself received: Jesus appeared to Cephas, that is Peter, to the Twelve, to more than five hundred at once, most of them still living, then to James, then to all the apostles, and last to Paul. This James is the brother of the Lord whom Paul met in Jerusalem and whose death Josephus records in AD 62.","refs":[{"label":"1 Corinthians 15:3–8","doc":"scripture/new-testament/07-1-corinthians","at":"chapter-15"}],"quote":{"ref":"1 Corinthians 15:6–7","text":"Then he appeared to over five hundred brothers at once, most of whom remain until now, but some have also fallen asleep. Then he appeared to James"},"witness":["scripture/new-testament/07-1-corinthians","scripture/new-testament/09-galatians","witnesses/josephus-antiquities-20"]},{"id":"ascension","title":"The ascension","when":"Forty days after (Acts 1:3)","place":"bethany","summary":"Luke ends his Gospel with Jesus leading the disciples out as far as Bethany, blessing them and being carried up into heaven. In Acts, his second volume, Luke tells of forty days of appearances, then Jesus taken up in a cloud from the Mount of Olives, with the promise that he will return.","refs":[{"label":"Luke 24:50–53","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-24"},{"label":"Acts 1:1–11","doc":"scripture/new-testament/05-acts","at":"chapter-1"}],"quote":null,"witness":["scripture/new-testament/05-acts"]},{"id":"pentecost","title":"Pentecost","when":"Fifty days after Passover","place":"jerusalem","summary":"At the feast of Pentecost the disciples are gathered in one place when a sound like a rushing wind fills the house and tongues as of fire rest on each of them. Peter stands before the crowds in Jerusalem and proclaims the crucified Jesus raised and exalted, and the church’s mission begins.","refs":[{"label":"Acts 2:1–41","doc":"scripture/new-testament/05-acts","at":"chapter-2"}],"quote":null,"witness":["scripture/new-testament/05-acts"]}]}]},"prophecies":{"lede":"Thirty-six passages from Israel’s scriptures set beside the New Testament scenes that read them as fulfilled in Jesus, from Bethlehem to the right hand of God.","intro":["The first Christians were Jews who read their own scriptures as pointing to Jesus. This page sets thirty-six of the best-known connections side by side: on the left the Old Testament passage, on the right the New Testament text that applies it to him. Each note gives the passage’s original setting in brief and how the New Testament reads it, so the reader can weigh both.","Each pair carries one of three labels. Explicit means the New Testament itself quotes the passage and applies it to Jesus, often with the words “that it might be fulfilled”. Allusion means the New Testament echoes the passage’s words or images without a formal citation. Typology means an earlier person, event or ritual is read as a pattern that foreshadows him."],"groups":[{"title":"His birth and line","art":"nativity-botticelli","items":[{"id":"born-of-a-virgin","title":"Born of a virgin","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Isaiah 7:14","text":"Therefore the Lord himself will give you a sign. Behold, the virgin will conceive, and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/isaiah","at":"chapter-7"},"nt":{"ref":"Matthew 1:22–23","text":"Now all this has happened that it might be fulfilled which was spoken by the Lord through the prophet, saying, “Behold, the virgin shall be with child, and shall give birth to a son. They shall call his name Immanuel,” which is, being interpreted, “God with us.”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-1"},"note":"Isaiah gave this sign to Ahaz, king of Judah, during a war crisis in the eighth century BC, and the child’s growth set a near horizon for it. The Hebrew word almah means a young woman of marriageable age, while the Greek translation Matthew follows has parthenos, “virgin”. Matthew reads the sign as reaching its fullness in Jesus, conceived by the Holy Spirit and named “God with us”.","cite":["P05","P06"]},{"id":"born-in-bethlehem","title":"Born in Bethlehem","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Micah 5:2","text":"But you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, being small among the clans of Judah, out of you one will come out to me who is to be ruler in Israel; whose goings out are from of old, from ancient times.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/micah","at":"chapter-5"},"nt":{"ref":"Matthew 2:4–6","text":"Gathering together all the chief priests and scribes of the people, he asked them where the Christ would be born. They said to him, “In Bethlehem of Judea, for this is written through the prophet, ‘You Bethlehem, land of Judah, are in no way least among the princes of Judah; for out of you shall come a governor who shall shepherd my people, Israel.’”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-2"},"note":"Micah, an eighth-century BC prophet, looked for a ruler from Bethlehem, the home town of King David, whose roots reach back to ancient times. In Matthew it is the chief priests and scribes who give this answer to Herod the Great, the Roman-backed king of Judea, joining Micah’s words to the shepherd language of 2 Samuel 5:2.","cite":["P07"]},{"id":"heir-to-davids-throne","title":"Heir to David’s throne","kind":"allusion","ot":{"ref":"2 Samuel 7:12–13","text":"When your days are fulfilled and you sleep with your fathers, I will set up your offspring after you, who will proceed out of your body, and I will establish his kingdom. He will build a house for my name, and I will establish the throne of his kingdom forever.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/2-samuel","at":"chapter-7"},"nt":{"ref":"Luke 1:31–33","text":"Behold, you will conceive in your womb and give birth to a son, and shall name him ‘Jesus.’ He will be great and will be called the Son of the Most High. The Lord God will give him the throne of his father David, and he will reign over the house of Jacob forever. There will be no end to his Kingdom.”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-1"},"note":"Through the prophet Nathan, God promised David a son who would build the temple and a dynasty that would last forever; its first fulfilment was Solomon and the kings after him. Luke’s angel speaks to Mary in the same terms, and Acts 2:30 and Romans 1:3 make the Davidic claim explicit."},{"id":"star-out-of-jacob","title":"A star out of Jacob","kind":"allusion","ot":{"ref":"Numbers 24:17","text":"I see him, but not now. I see him, but not near. A star will come out of Jacob. A scepter will rise out of Israel …","doc":"scripture/old-testament/numbers","at":"chapter-24"},"nt":{"ref":"Matthew 2:1–2","text":"Now when Jesus was born in Bethlehem of Judea in the days of King Herod, behold, wise men from the east came to Jerusalem, saying, “Where is he who is born King of the Jews? For we saw his star in the east, and have come to worship him.”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-2"},"note":"Balaam, a foreign seer hired to curse Israel, instead foresaw a ruler rising from Jacob like a star. Matthew does not quote the oracle, but the star that leads eastern wise men to a newborn king draws on the same royal image, and Revelation 22:16 calls Jesus “the bright and morning star”."},{"id":"out-of-egypt","title":"Called out of Egypt","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Hosea 11:1","text":"“When Israel was a child, then I loved him, and called my son out of Egypt.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/hosea","at":"chapter-11"},"nt":{"ref":"Matthew 2:14–15","text":"He arose and took the young child and his mother by night and departed into Egypt, and was there until the death of Herod, that it might be fulfilled which was spoken by the Lord through the prophet, saying, “Out of Egypt I called my son.”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-2"},"note":"Hosea looks back to the exodus, when God called Israel, his son, out of slavery. Matthew uses his fulfilment formula for a pattern rather than a forecast: Jesus, the Son, relives Israel’s story, going down into Egypt and coming out again.","cite":["P42"]},{"id":"rachel-weeping","title":"Rachel weeping for her children","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Jeremiah 31:15","text":"Yahweh says: “A voice is heard in Ramah, lamentation and bitter weeping, Rachel weeping for her children. She refuses to be comforted for her children, because they are no more.”","doc":"scripture/old-testament/jeremiah","at":"chapter-31"},"nt":{"ref":"Matthew 2:16–18","text":"Then Herod … sent out and killed all the male children who were in Bethlehem and in all the surrounding countryside, from two years old and under … Then that which was spoken by Jeremiah the prophet was fulfilled, saying, “A voice was heard in Ramah, lamentation, weeping and great mourning, Rachel weeping for her children; she wouldn’t be comforted, because they are no more.”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-2"},"note":"Rachel, wife of the patriarch Jacob and mother of Israel’s tribes, was buried on the road to Bethlehem; Jeremiah pictures her mourning the exiles of his own day, and the following verses promise their return. Matthew hears her lament again in Herod’s killing of the Bethlehem children."}]},{"title":"His ministry","art":"baptism","items":[{"id":"voice-in-the-wilderness","title":"A voice in the wilderness","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Isaiah 40:3","text":"The voice of one who calls out, “Prepare the way of Yahweh in the wilderness! Make a level highway in the desert for our God.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/isaiah","at":"chapter-40"},"nt":{"ref":"Matthew 3:1–3","text":"In those days, John the Baptizer came, preaching in the wilderness of Judea, saying, “Repent, for the Kingdom of Heaven is at hand!” For this is he who was spoken of by Isaiah the prophet, saying, “The voice of one crying in the wilderness, make the way of the Lord ready! Make his paths straight!”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-3"},"note":"Isaiah announces a highway through the desert for God to lead the exiles home from Babylon. All four Gospels apply the voice to John the Baptist, the desert preacher who baptised Jesus, so that the way prepared for Israel’s God becomes the way prepared for Jesus."},{"id":"light-in-galilee","title":"A great light in Galilee","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Isaiah 9:1–2","text":"In the former time, he brought into contempt the land of Zebulun and the land of Naphtali; but in the latter time he has made it glorious, by the way of the sea, beyond the Jordan, Galilee of the nations. The people who walked in darkness have seen a great light. The light has shined on those who lived in the land of the shadow of death.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/isaiah","at":"chapter-9"},"nt":{"ref":"Matthew 4:13–16","text":"Leaving Nazareth, he came and lived in Capernaum, which is by the sea, in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali, that it might be fulfilled which was spoken through Isaiah the prophet, saying, “The land of Zebulun and the land of Naphtali, toward the sea, beyond the Jordan, Galilee of the Gentiles, the people who sat in darkness saw a great light; to those who sat in the region and shadow of death, to them light has dawned.”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-4"},"note":"Zebulun and Naphtali, the northern tribal lands around the Sea of Galilee, had been humbled in war; Isaiah promises that they will be the first to see the light of deliverance. Matthew finds that promise in Jesus making Capernaum, a fishing town on the lake, the base of his ministry."},{"id":"good-news-to-the-poor","title":"Good news to the poor","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Isaiah 61:1–2","text":"The Lord Yahweh’s Spirit is on me, because Yahweh has anointed me to preach good news to the humble. He has sent me to bind up the broken hearted, to proclaim liberty to the captives and release to those who are bound, to proclaim the year of Yahweh’s favor …","doc":"scripture/old-testament/isaiah","at":"chapter-61"},"nt":{"ref":"Luke 4:17–21","text":"The book of the prophet Isaiah was handed to him. He opened the book, and found the place where it was written, “The Spirit of the Lord is on me, because he has anointed me to preach good news to the poor. He has sent me to heal the broken hearted, to proclaim release to the captives, recovering of sight to the blind, to deliver those who are crushed, and to proclaim the acceptable year of the Lord.” He closed the book, gave it back to the attendant, and sat down. … He began to tell them, “Today, this Scripture has been fulfilled in your hearing.”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-4"},"note":"Isaiah’s anointed herald proclaims release and comfort to the afflicted of Zion. In the synagogue of Nazareth, his home town, Jesus reads the passage, blending in a line from Isaiah 58:6 and stopping before “the day of vengeance of our God”, and declares it fulfilled that day.","cite":["P10","P11"]},{"id":"bore-our-sicknesses","title":"He took our infirmities","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Isaiah 53:4","text":"Surely he has borne our sickness and carried our suffering …","doc":"scripture/old-testament/isaiah","at":"chapter-53"},"nt":{"ref":"Matthew 8:16–17","text":"When evening came, they brought to him many possessed with demons. He cast out the spirits with a word, and healed all who were sick, that it might be fulfilled which was spoken through Isaiah the prophet, saying, “He took our infirmities and bore our diseases.”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-8"},"note":"This line comes from Isaiah’s poem of the suffering servant (Isaiah 52:13–53:12). Matthew applies it first not to the cross but to an evening of healing in Capernaum, where Jesus takes the people’s sicknesses on himself."},{"id":"lifted-up-serpent","title":"The serpent lifted up","kind":"typology","ot":{"ref":"Numbers 21:8–9","text":"Yahweh said to Moses, “Make a venomous snake, and set it on a pole. It shall happen that everyone who is bitten, when he sees it, shall live.” Moses made a serpent of bronze, and set it on the pole. If a serpent had bitten any man, when he looked at the serpent of bronze, he lived.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/numbers","at":"chapter-21"},"nt":{"ref":"John 3:14–15","text":"As Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, even so must the Son of Man be lifted up, that whoever believes in him should not perish, but have eternal life.","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-3"},"note":"In the wilderness, Israelites bitten by snakes lived when they looked at the bronze serpent Moses set on a pole. Speaking to Nicodemus, a Pharisee and member of the Jerusalem council, Jesus takes the scene as a figure of his own lifting up on the cross, and of the life that comes from looking to him."}]},{"title":"Entry to Jerusalem and betrayal","art":"last-supper","items":[{"id":"king-on-a-donkey","title":"The king on a donkey","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Zechariah 9:9","text":"Rejoice greatly, daughter of Zion! Shout, daughter of Jerusalem! Behold, your King comes to you! He is righteous, and having salvation; lowly, and riding on a donkey, even on a colt, the foal of a donkey.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/zechariah","at":"chapter-9"},"nt":{"ref":"Matthew 21:4–5","text":"All this was done that it might be fulfilled which was spoken through the prophet, saying, “Tell the daughter of Zion, behold, your King comes to you, humble, and riding on a donkey, on a colt, the foal of a donkey.”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-21"},"note":"Zechariah, a prophet of the return from exile, pictures Jerusalem’s king arriving humbly, bringing peace to the nations. Jesus deliberately acts the sign out, sending for the animal and riding into the city at Passover; Matthew names both a donkey and a colt where Mark has one colt, following the doubled line of the Hebrew poem.","cite":["P17"]},{"id":"rejected-stone","title":"The stone the builders rejected","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Psalm 118:22–23","text":"The stone which the builders rejected has become the cornerstone. This is Yahweh’s doing. It is marvelous in our eyes.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/psalms","at":"psalm-118"},"nt":{"ref":"Mark 12:10–11","text":"Haven’t you even read this Scripture: ‘The stone which the builders rejected was made the head of the corner. This was from the Lord. It is marvelous in our eyes’?”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-12"},"note":"Psalm 118, sung by pilgrims at the festivals, celebrates a reversal: what was thrown aside becomes the stone that holds the building together. Jesus quotes it in the temple at the close of his parable of the wicked tenants, and Peter returns to it in Acts 4:11 and 1 Peter 2:7."},{"id":"betrayed-by-a-friend","title":"Betrayed by a friend at table","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Psalm 41:9","text":"Yes, my own familiar friend, in whom I trusted, who ate bread with me, has lifted up his heel against me.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/psalms","at":"psalm-41"},"nt":{"ref":"John 13:18","text":"I don’t speak concerning all of you. I know whom I have chosen; but that the Scripture may be fulfilled, ‘He who eats bread with me has lifted up his heel against me.’","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-13"},"note":"The psalmist, sick and surrounded by enemies, grieves most over a trusted friend who shared his bread and turned against him. At the Last Supper Jesus applies the line to Judas Iscariot, one of the Twelve, who would hand him over that night."},{"id":"thirty-pieces-of-silver","title":"Thirty pieces of silver","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Zechariah 11:12–13","text":"I said to them, “If you think it best, give me my wages; and if not, keep them.” So they weighed for my wages thirty pieces of silver. Yahweh said to me, “Throw it to the potter—the handsome price that I was valued at by them!” I took the thirty pieces of silver and threw them to the potter in Yahweh’s house.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/zechariah","at":"chapter-11"},"nt":{"ref":"Matthew 27:9–10","text":"Then that which was spoken through Jeremiah the prophet was fulfilled, saying, “They took the thirty pieces of silver, the price of him upon whom a price had been set, whom some of the children of Israel priced, and they gave them for the potter’s field, as the Lord commanded me.”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-27"},"note":"Zechariah, acting the part of a shepherd rejected by the flock, is paid an insulting wage of thirty pieces of silver and throws it to the potter in the temple. Matthew sees the same price in Judas’s payment, flung back into the temple and spent on a potter’s field. Matthew names Jeremiah, though the clearest wording is Zechariah’s; the usual explanation is that he blends Jeremiah’s passages about a potter and a field.","cite":["P18"]},{"id":"shepherd-struck","title":"The shepherd struck, the sheep scattered","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Zechariah 13:7","text":"“Awake, sword, against my shepherd, and against the man who is close to me,” says Yahweh of Armies. “Strike the shepherd, and the sheep will be scattered …","doc":"scripture/old-testament/zechariah","at":"chapter-13"},"nt":{"ref":"Mark 14:27","text":"Jesus said to them, “All of you will be made to stumble because of me tonight, for it is written, ‘I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep will be scattered.’","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-14"},"note":"Zechariah’s oracle speaks of God’s own shepherd struck down and the flock scattered before a people is refined. On the way to Gethsemane, the olive grove where he was arrested, Jesus applies it to himself and his disciples, who that night all fled."}]},{"title":"His suffering and death","art":"mond-crucifixion","items":[{"id":"silent-as-a-lamb","title":"Silent as a lamb","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Isaiah 53:7","text":"He was oppressed, yet when he was afflicted he didn’t open his mouth. As a lamb that is led to the slaughter, and as a sheep that before its shearers is silent, so he didn’t open his mouth.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/isaiah","at":"chapter-53"},"nt":{"ref":"Acts 8:32–35","text":"Now the passage of the Scripture which he was reading was this, “He was led as a sheep to the slaughter. As a lamb before his shearer is silent, so he doesn’t open his mouth. … The eunuch answered Philip, “Who is the prophet talking about? About himself, or about someone else?” Philip opened his mouth, and beginning from this Scripture, preached to him about Jesus.","doc":"scripture/new-testament/05-acts","at":"chapter-8"},"note":"Isaiah’s servant goes to his death without protest. The Gospels record Jesus keeping silence before the high priest and before Pilate (Mark 14:61; 15:5), and in Acts Philip, one of the seven chosen to serve the Jerusalem church, explains the passage to an Ethiopian court official as a word about Jesus.","cite":["P25"]},{"id":"counted-with-transgressors","title":"Counted with the transgressors","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Isaiah 53:12","text":"… because he poured out his soul to death and was counted with the transgressors; yet he bore the sins of many and made intercession for the transgressors.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/isaiah","at":"chapter-53"},"nt":{"ref":"Luke 22:37","text":"For I tell you that this which is written must still be fulfilled in me: ‘He was counted with transgressors.’","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-22"},"note":"The servant poem ends with one who dies among the guilty while bearing their sins and praying for them. On the night of his arrest Jesus quotes the line of himself, and Luke goes on to tell of his crucifixion between two criminals (Luke 23:32–33).","cite":["P43"]},{"id":"hands-and-feet","title":"His hands and feet","kind":"allusion","ot":{"ref":"Psalm 22:16–17","text":"For dogs have surrounded me. A company of evildoers have enclosed me. They have pierced my hands and feet. I can count all of my bones. They look and stare at me.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/psalms","at":"psalm-22"},"nt":{"ref":"John 20:25–27","text":"But he said to them, “Unless I see in his hands the print of the nails, put my finger into the print of the nails, and put my hand into his side, I will not believe.” … Then he said to Thomas, “Reach here your finger, and see my hands. Reach here your hand, and put it into my side. Don’t be unbelieving, but believing.”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-20"},"note":"Psalm 22 is the lament of a man surrounded by enemies who is finally rescued and praises God. The received Hebrew text reads “like a lion” at this point, while the ancient Greek translation has a verb meaning “dug” or “pierced”, and the reading is debated. The New Testament does not quote this line, but its accounts of the nail wounds shown to Thomas, the doubting disciple, led Justin Martyr, the second-century Christian writer, to make the connection explicit.","cite":["P20","P21"]},{"id":"lots-for-his-clothing","title":"Lots cast for his clothing","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Psalm 22:18","text":"They divide my garments among them. They cast lots for my clothing.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/psalms","at":"psalm-22"},"nt":{"ref":"John 19:23–24","text":"Then the soldiers, when they had crucified Jesus, took his garments and made four parts, to every soldier a part; and also the tunic. Now the tunic was without seam, woven from the top throughout. Then they said to one another, “Let’s not tear it, but cast lots for it to decide whose it will be,” that the Scripture might be fulfilled, which says, “They parted my garments among them. They cast lots for my clothing.”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-19"},"note":"In the psalm the sufferer’s enemies share out his clothes as though he were already dead. All four Gospels report the soldiers dividing Jesus’s garments, and John, who quotes the verse, sees its two parallel lines in two acts: the garments shared out, and lots cast for the seamless tunic.","cite":["P22"]},{"id":"vinegar-for-thirst","title":"Vinegar for his thirst","kind":"allusion","ot":{"ref":"Psalm 69:21","text":"They also gave me poison for my food. In my thirst, they gave me vinegar to drink.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/psalms","at":"psalm-69"},"nt":{"ref":"John 19:28–30","text":"After this, Jesus, seeing that all things were now finished, that the Scripture might be fulfilled, said, “I am thirsty!” Now a vessel full of vinegar was set there; so they put a sponge full of the vinegar on hyssop, and held it at his mouth. When Jesus therefore had received the vinegar, he said, “It is finished!”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-19"},"note":"Psalm 69 is the cry of an innocent man insulted by those around him, given poison and vinegar in place of food and drink. John says Jesus spoke of his thirst so that Scripture would be fulfilled without naming the passage; this psalm, with Psalm 22:15, is the likely background, and the Gospels each tell of the sour wine offered at the cross."},{"id":"my-god-why-have-you-forsaken-me","title":"“My God, why have you forsaken me?”","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Psalm 22:1","text":"My God, my God, why have you forsaken me? Why are you so far from helping me, and from the words of my groaning?","doc":"scripture/old-testament/psalms","at":"psalm-22"},"nt":{"ref":"Mark 15:34","text":"At the ninth hour Jesus cried with a loud voice, saying, “Eloi, Eloi, lama sabachthani?”which is, being interpreted, “My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-15"},"note":"Jesus cries the opening words of Psalm 22 from the cross in Aramaic, the language he spoke. The psalm moves from desolation to public praise and the nations turning to God (Psalm 22:22–31), and readers have long debated whether the cry voices only the anguish or also calls to mind the whole psalm’s ending.","cite":["P44"]},{"id":"no-bone-broken","title":"Not one bone broken","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Exodus 12:46; Psalm 34:20","text":"… Do not break any of its bones. … He protects all of his bones. Not one of them is broken.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/exodus","at":"chapter-12"},"nt":{"ref":"John 19:33–36","text":"but when they came to Jesus and saw that he was already dead, they didn’t break his legs. … For these things happened that the Scripture might be fulfilled, “A bone of him will not be broken.”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-19"},"note":"The Passover law forbade breaking any bone of the lamb, and Psalm 34 promises that God guards every bone of the righteous. When the soldiers break the legs of the men crucified with Jesus but find him already dead, John sees both texts fulfilled at once: Jesus is the righteous one and the true Passover lamb.","cite":["P22"]},{"id":"they-will-look-on-him","title":"They will look on him whom they pierced","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Zechariah 12:10","text":"I will pour on David’s house and on the inhabitants of Jerusalem the spirit of grace and of supplication. They will look to me whom they have pierced; and they shall mourn for him as one mourns for his only son …","doc":"scripture/old-testament/zechariah","at":"chapter-12"},"nt":{"ref":"John 19:34–37","text":"However, one of the soldiers pierced his side with a spear, and immediately blood and water came out. … Again another Scripture says, “They will look on him whom they pierced.”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-19"},"note":"Zechariah foresees Jerusalem, given a new spirit, mourning over one it has pierced as a family mourns an only son. The Hebrew reads “look to me” where John has “look on him”, and the shift in pronouns is discussed. John applies the verse to the soldier’s spear thrust, and Revelation 1:7 to the day when every eye will see him.","cite":["P23"]},{"id":"grave-with-the-rich","title":"His grave with a rich man","kind":"allusion","ot":{"ref":"Isaiah 53:9","text":"They made his grave with the wicked, and with a rich man in his death, although he had done no violence, nor was any deceit in his mouth.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/isaiah","at":"chapter-53"},"nt":{"ref":"Matthew 27:57–60","text":"When evening had come, a rich man from Arimathaea named Joseph, who himself was also Jesus’ disciple, came. This man went to Pilate and asked for Jesus’ body. … Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean linen cloth and laid it in his own new tomb, which he had cut out in the rock.","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-27"},"note":"Isaiah’s servant, though innocent, is buried as one of the wicked and “with a rich man”; the Hebrew of this line is difficult and translations vary. Matthew alone calls Joseph of Arimathea rich, and tells how he laid Jesus in his own new rock-cut tomb after asking Pilate, the Roman governor, for the body.","cite":["P24"]},{"id":"pierced-for-our-transgressions","title":"Wounded for our transgressions","kind":"allusion","ot":{"ref":"Isaiah 53:5–6","text":"But he was pierced for our transgressions. He was crushed for our iniquities. The punishment that brought our peace was on him; and by his wounds we are healed. All we like sheep have gone astray. Everyone has turned to his own way; and Yahweh has laid on him the iniquity of us all.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/isaiah","at":"chapter-53"},"nt":{"ref":"1 Peter 2:24–25","text":"He himself bore our sins in his body on the tree, that we, having died to sins, might live to righteousness. You were healed by his wounds. For you were going astray like sheep; but now you have returned to the Shepherd and Overseer of your souls.","doc":"scripture/new-testament/21-1-peter","at":"chapter-2"},"note":"At the heart of Isaiah’s servant poem, onlookers realise that the one they despised was suffering in their place. Whether the servant is the prophet, Israel or a single figure to come is an old question of interpretation. 1 Peter weaves the poem’s words into its account of the cross without a formal citation, and this reading of the servant became central to the Christian understanding of Jesus’s death.","cite":["P25"]},{"id":"passover-lamb","title":"The Passover lamb","kind":"typology","ot":{"ref":"Exodus 12:5–7","text":"Your lamb shall be without defect, a male a year old. … They shall take some of the blood, and put it on the two door posts and on the lintel, on the houses in which they shall eat it.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/exodus","at":"chapter-12"},"nt":{"ref":"1 Corinthians 5:7","text":"Purge out the old yeast, that you may be a new lump, even as you are unleavened. For indeed Christ, our Passover, has been sacrificed in our place.","doc":"scripture/new-testament/07-1-corinthians","at":"chapter-5"},"note":"On the night of the exodus, the blood of a spotless lamb marked the houses God passed over and spared. Paul calls Christ “our Passover”, John the Baptist hails him as “the Lamb of God” (John 1:29), and John’s Gospel places his death on the day the Passover lambs were prepared."}]},{"title":"Resurrection and exaltation","art":"resurrection","items":[{"id":"sign-of-jonah","title":"The sign of Jonah","kind":"typology","ot":{"ref":"Jonah 1:17","text":"Yahweh prepared a huge fish to swallow up Jonah, and Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/jonah","at":"chapter-1"},"nt":{"ref":"Matthew 12:39–40","text":"But he answered them, “An evil and adulterous generation seeks after a sign, but no sign will be given to it but the sign of Jonah the prophet. For as Jonah was three days and three nights in the belly of the huge fish, so will the Son of Man be three days and three nights in the heart of the earth.","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-12"},"note":"Jonah, the prophet who fled God’s call, was swallowed by a great fish and brought out alive on the third day. Asked for a sign, Jesus offers only this one: his own time in the grave and return. The span from Friday to Sunday follows the ancient habit of counting part days as whole ones."},{"id":"not-abandoned-to-the-grave","title":"Not abandoned to the grave","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Psalm 16:10","text":"For you will not leave my soul in Sheol, neither will you allow your holy one to see corruption.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/psalms","at":"psalm-16"},"nt":{"ref":"Acts 2:29–32","text":"“Brothers, I may tell you freely of the patriarch David, that he both died and was buried, and his tomb is with us to this day. … he foreseeing this, spoke about the resurrection of the Christ, that his soul wasn’t left in Hades, and his flesh didn’t see decay. This Jesus God raised up, to which we all are witnesses.","doc":"scripture/new-testament/05-acts","at":"chapter-2"},"note":"The psalmist trusts that God will not hand him over to Sheol, the realm of the dead; whether he first meant rescue from death or life beyond it is debated. Peter at Pentecost, and Paul in Acts 13:35–37, argue that David died and his tomb is still there, so the words find their full sense in Jesus raised from the dead.","cite":["P31"]},{"id":"you-are-my-son","title":"“You are my Son”","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Psalm 2:7","text":"I will tell of the decree: Yahweh said to me, “You are my son. Today I have become your father.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/psalms","at":"psalm-2"},"nt":{"ref":"Acts 13:32–33","text":"We bring you good news of the promise made to the fathers, that God has fulfilled this to us, their children, in that he raised up Jesus. As it is also written in the second psalm, ‘You are my Son. Today I have become your father.’","doc":"scripture/new-testament/05-acts","at":"chapter-13"},"note":"Psalm 2 is a royal psalm, probably sung when a king of Judah was enthroned and God declared him his son. Preaching in a synagogue in Asia Minor, Paul reads the decree as spoken over Jesus at his resurrection, and Hebrews 1:5 uses it to set the Son above the angels.","cite":["P04"]},{"id":"at-gods-right-hand","title":"At God’s right hand","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Psalm 110:1","text":"A Psalm by David. Yahweh says to my Lord, “Sit at my right hand, until I make your enemies your footstool for your feet.”","doc":"scripture/old-testament/psalms","at":"psalm-110"},"nt":{"ref":"Acts 2:34–36","text":"For David didn’t ascend into the heavens, but he says himself, ‘The Lord said to my Lord, “Sit by my right hand until I make your enemies a footstool for your feet.”’ “Let all the house of Israel therefore know certainly that God has made him both Lord and Christ, this Jesus whom you crucified.”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/05-acts","at":"chapter-2"},"note":"A royal psalm in which God seats the king beside him in the place of honour. Jesus himself asks how David can call his own descendant “Lord” (Mark 12:35–37), and from Peter’s first sermon onward it is one of the passages the New Testament returns to most often to describe his exaltation.","cite":["P32"]},{"id":"son-of-man-on-the-clouds","title":"The Son of Man on the clouds","kind":"allusion","ot":{"ref":"Daniel 7:13–14","text":"“I saw in the night visions, and behold, there came with the clouds of the sky one like a son of man, and he came even to the Ancient of Days, and they brought him near before him. Dominion was given him, and glory, and a kingdom, that all the peoples, nations, and languages should serve him. …","doc":"scripture/old-testament/daniel","at":"chapter-7"},"nt":{"ref":"Mark 14:61–62","text":"Again the high priest asked him, “Are you the Christ, the Son of the Blessed?” Jesus said, “I am. You will see the Son of Man sitting at the right hand of Power, and coming with the clouds of the sky.”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-14"},"note":"In Daniel’s vision the empires of the world appear as beasts, and then one like a son of man receives an everlasting kingdom from God, the Ancient of Days. Whether the figure was first an individual or stood for “the holy ones” of Israel is debated. On trial before the high priest Caiaphas, Jesus joins Daniel’s words to Psalm 110, and it is this answer that brings the charge of blasphemy.","cite":["P33"]}]},{"title":"The nations and the kingdom","art":"pentecost","items":[{"id":"blessing-for-the-nations","title":"A blessing for all nations","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Genesis 12:3","text":"I will bless those who bless you, and I will curse him who treats you with contempt. All the families of the earth will be blessed through you.”","doc":"scripture/old-testament/genesis","at":"chapter-12"},"nt":{"ref":"Galatians 3:8","text":"The Scripture, foreseeing that God would justify the Gentiles by faith, preached the Good News beforehand to Abraham, saying, “In you all the nations will be blessed.”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/09-galatians","at":"chapter-3"},"note":"When God called Abraham, he promised that through him and his descendants all the families of the earth would be blessed. Paul reads that promise as the gospel announced in advance, fulfilled as non-Jews come to share Abraham’s blessing through faith in Christ.","cite":["P02"]},{"id":"light-to-the-nations","title":"A light to the nations","kind":"allusion","ot":{"ref":"Isaiah 49:6","text":"Indeed, he says, “It is too light a thing that you should be my servant to raise up the tribes of Jacob, and to restore the preserved of Israel. I will also give you as a light to the nations, that you may be my salvation to the end of the earth.”","doc":"scripture/old-testament/isaiah","at":"chapter-49"},"nt":{"ref":"Luke 2:29–32","text":"“Now you are releasing your servant, Master, according to your word, in peace; for my eyes have seen your salvation, which you have prepared before the face of all peoples; a light for revelation to the nations, and the glory of your people Israel.”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-2"},"note":"God tells his servant that restoring Israel is too small a task: he is to be a light to the nations and salvation to the end of the earth. Simeon, an old man waiting in the temple for Israel’s comfort, takes the infant Jesus in his arms and greets him in Isaiah’s words; in Acts 13:47 Paul and Barnabas take the same commission for their mission.","cite":["P14"]},{"id":"new-covenant","title":"A new covenant","kind":"allusion","ot":{"ref":"Jeremiah 31:31–33","text":"“Behold, the days come,” says Yahweh, “that I will make a new covenant with the house of Israel, and with the house of Judah, … I will put my law in their inward parts, and I will write it in their heart. I will be their God, and they shall be my people.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/jeremiah","at":"chapter-31"},"nt":{"ref":"Luke 22:20","text":"Likewise, he took the cup after supper, saying, “This cup is the new covenant in my blood, which is poured out for you.","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-22"},"note":"Jeremiah, the prophet of Jerusalem’s fall to Babylon, promised a covenant written on the heart, with sins forgiven and remembered no more. At the Last Supper Jesus calls the cup “the new covenant in my blood”, and Hebrews 8:8–12 quotes Jeremiah’s promise in full as fulfilled in him.","cite":["P27"]},{"id":"spirit-poured-out","title":"The Spirit poured out","kind":"explicit","ot":{"ref":"Joel 2:28–29","text":"“It will happen afterward, that I will pour out my Spirit on all flesh; and your sons and your daughters will prophesy. Your old men will dream dreams. Your young men will see visions. And also on the servants and on the handmaids in those days, I will pour out my Spirit.","doc":"scripture/old-testament/joel","at":"chapter-2"},"nt":{"ref":"Acts 2:16–17","text":"But this is what has been spoken through the prophet Joel: ‘It will be in the last days, says God, that I will pour out my Spirit on all flesh. Your sons and your daughters will prophesy. Your young men will see visions. Your old men will dream dreams.","doc":"scripture/new-testament/05-acts","at":"chapter-2"},"note":"Joel promised that God’s Spirit, once given to a few prophets, would be poured out on young and old, slave and free. At Pentecost, the Jewish harvest festival fifty days after Passover, Peter tells the crowd in Jerusalem that this is what they are witnessing in the disciples, poured out, he says, by the exalted Jesus (Acts 2:33)."}]}]},"resurrection":{"lede":"Eight lines of evidence, drawn from the Gospels, Paul, Roman and Jewish writers and the first Christian leaders, set side by side for the reader to weigh.","intro":["Christianity stands or falls on one claim: that Jesus of Nazareth, crucified under Pontius Pilate, the Roman governor of Judea, was raised from the dead on the third day. Paul put it bluntly to the church at Corinth: if Christ has not been raised, the faith is empty.","The evidence for that claim is spread across many documents in this archive. This study gathers it in order, from the death and burial to the empty tomb, the appearances, the sceptics who changed sides and the first witnesses who suffered for what they proclaimed. Each line quotes its sources directly, so the reader can check every step."],"lines":[{"id":"death-by-crucifixion","title":"He died by crucifixion","claim":"Jesus was executed by Roman crucifixion under Pontius Pilate, a fact reported by Christian, Roman and Jewish sources alike.","body":["All four Gospels narrate the arrest, the hearing before Pilate and the crucifixion outside Jerusalem. Their accounts agree on the essentials: a Roman execution, carried out on the eve of a Sabbath at Passover time, with Jesus dead before evening. John adds that a soldier pierced his side to make sure of it.","Writers with no sympathy for Christianity say the same. Tacitus, the Roman historian, writing c. AD 116, records that Christus suffered the death penalty under Tiberius by sentence of Pilate. Josephus, the Jewish historian, writing c. AD 93–94, reports that Pilate condemned Jesus to the cross; the passage was later expanded by Christian copyists, but most scholars hold that its core, including the execution, is his.","Two further sources point the same way. Mara bar Serapion, a Syrian prisoner writing to his son at a debated date, speaks of the Jews putting their “wise king” to death. A tradition in the Babylonian Talmud, Sanhedrin 43a, says Jesus the Nazarene was hanged on Passover eve. Hostile and friendly witnesses agree: Jesus died, publicly, at the hands of the authorities."],"texts":[{"ref":"Mark 15:24–25, 37","text":"Crucifying him, they parted his garments among them, casting lots on them, what each should take. It was the third hour when they crucified him. … Jesus cried out with a loud voice, and gave up the spirit.","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-15"},{"ref":"Tacitus, Annals 15.44","text":"Christus, the founder of the name, had undergone the death penalty in the reign of Tiberius, by sentence of the procurator Pontius Pilatus","doc":"witnesses/tacitus-annals-15-44","at":"annals-15-44"},{"ref":"Josephus, Antiquities 18.63–64","text":"when Pilate, at the suggestion of the principal men among us, had condemned him to the cross; those that loved him at the first did not forsake him.","doc":"witnesses/josephus-antiquities-18","at":"ant-18-3-3"},{"ref":"Babylonian Talmud, Sanhedrin 43a","text":"On Passover Eve they hung the corpse of Jesus the Nazarene after they killed him by way of stoning.","doc":"witnesses/talmud-sanhedrin-43a","at":"the-baraita-about-yeshu"}],"links":["witnesses/tacitus-annals-15-44","witnesses/josephus-antiquities-18","witnesses/mara-bar-serapion","witnesses/talmud-sanhedrin-43a","archaeology/pilate-inscription"],"art":"mond-crucifixion"},{"id":"burial","title":"He was buried","claim":"Jesus’s body was placed in a known rock-cut tomb by Joseph of Arimathea, and the earliest Christian creed records that “he was buried”.","body":["All four Gospels name the man who buried Jesus: Joseph of Arimathea, a town in Judea, described by Mark as a prominent member of the council, the Jewish ruling body in Jerusalem that had handed Jesus to Pilate. Joseph asked Pilate for the body, wrapped it in linen and laid it in a tomb cut from rock, closing it with a stone.","A named member of the very council that condemned Jesus is an unlikely figure for his followers to invent, and a public figure in Jerusalem would have been easy to check. Mark adds that Mary Magdalene and another Mary watched and “saw where he was laid”, so the place was known.","The creed Paul quotes in 1 Corinthians 15, which he had received years before he wrote it, includes the burial as its own clause: Christ died, he was buried, he was raised. The burial is not a detail added later; it stands in the oldest summary of the faith."],"texts":[{"ref":"Mark 15:43, 46–47","text":"Joseph of Arimathaea, a prominent council member who also himself was looking for God’s Kingdom, came. He boldly went in to Pilate, and asked for Jesus’ body. … He bought a linen cloth, and taking him down, wound him in the linen cloth and laid him in a tomb which had been cut out of a rock. He rolled a stone against the door of the tomb. Mary Magdalene and Mary the mother of Joses, saw where he was laid.","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-15"},{"ref":"1 Corinthians 15:3–4","text":"that Christ died for our sins according to the Scriptures, that he was buried, that he was raised on the third day according to the Scriptures","doc":"scripture/new-testament/07-1-corinthians","at":"chapter-15"}],"links":["scripture/new-testament/02-mark","scripture/new-testament/04-john","scripture/new-testament/07-1-corinthians"],"art":"entombment"},{"id":"empty-tomb","title":"The tomb was found empty","claim":"On the first day of the week the tomb was found empty, a fact the earliest opponents of the Christians did not deny but tried to explain.","body":["Mark, Matthew, Luke and John each report that the tomb was found open and empty early on the Sunday morning. They differ in the details of who went and what they saw first, as independent accounts of a confusing dawn tend to, but they agree on the central fact: the body was gone.","Matthew records the answer given by the authorities in Jerusalem: the disciples came by night and stole the body. He says this story was still told among the Jews in his own day. The counter-story concedes the point that matters. No one claimed the body was still in the tomb; the argument was over how it came to be empty.","The proclamation began in Jerusalem itself, a short walk from the tomb, within weeks of the execution. In Acts 2 Peter contrasts King David, whose tomb was still there to be seen, with Jesus, whom God raised. A message of that kind could not have survived in that city if the body could be produced."],"texts":[{"ref":"Mark 16:5–6","text":"Entering into the tomb, they saw a young man sitting on the right side, dressed in a white robe; and they were amazed. He said to them, “Don’t be amazed. You seek Jesus, the Nazarene, who has been crucified. He has risen! He is not here. See the place where they laid him!","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark","at":"chapter-16"},{"ref":"Matthew 28:12–13, 15","text":"they gave a large amount of silver to the soldiers, saying, “Say that his disciples came by night and stole him away while we slept. … This saying was spread abroad among the Jews, and continues until today.","doc":"scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","at":"chapter-28"},{"ref":"Acts 2:29, 32","text":"he both died and was buried, and his tomb is with us to this day. … This Jesus God raised up, to which we all are witnesses.","doc":"scripture/new-testament/05-acts","at":"chapter-2"}],"links":["scripture/new-testament/01-matthew","scripture/new-testament/03-luke","scripture/new-testament/04-john","scripture/new-testament/05-acts"],"art":"resurrection"},{"id":"women-first-witnesses","title":"Women were the first witnesses","claim":"Every Gospel names women, led by Mary Magdalene, as the first to find the tomb empty, at a time when a woman’s testimony carried little weight.","body":["In all four Gospels the discovery is made by women: Mary Magdalene, a follower of Jesus from Magdala in Galilee, with Mary the mother of James, Salome, Joanna and others. In John, Mary Magdalene is the first to see the risen Jesus and the one who carries the news to the disciples.","In first-century Judaism a woman’s word counted for little in public matters. Josephus states that women were not to be admitted as witnesses (Antiquities 4.219), and in the second century Celsus, a pagan critic, mocked the resurrection as resting on the word of an hysterical woman (reported by the third-century scholar Origen, Against Celsus 2.55). Luke admits that the apostles themselves at first dismissed the women’s report as nonsense.","Writers inventing a story to persuade would not have chosen witnesses their audience was inclined to discount. The formal creed Paul quotes, meant for public recitation, names only men. The women stand in the Gospel accounts because that is how the first Christians remembered it happening."],"texts":[{"ref":"Luke 24:10–11","text":"Now they were Mary Magdalene, Joanna, and Mary the mother of James. The other women with them told these things to the apostles. These words seemed to them to be nonsense, and they didn’t believe them.","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke","at":"chapter-24"},{"ref":"John 20:18","text":"Mary Magdalene came and told the disciples that she had seen the Lord, and that he had said these things to her.","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-20"}],"links":["scripture/new-testament/03-luke","scripture/new-testament/04-john","fathers/origen-contra-celsum-1"],"art":"giotto-lamentation"},{"id":"appearances","title":"The appearances","claim":"Individuals and groups, including Peter, the Twelve, more than five hundred at once, James and Paul, reported that they had seen Jesus alive after his death.","body":["Paul wrote 1 Corinthians c. AD 53–55, but the list of appearances in chapter 15 is older than the letter. He introduces it with the words for handing on fixed tradition, “delivered” and “received”, and its balanced clauses read like a formula learned by heart. It names Cephas (the Aramaic name of Peter), the Twelve, more than five hundred brothers at once, James, all the apostles, and finally Paul himself.","Paul tells the Galatians when he was in a position to receive this. Three years after his conversion he went to Jerusalem and stayed fifteen days with Peter, and he also met James, the brother of Jesus. That visit falls c. AD 35–37, within a few years of the crucifixion. The creed reaches back to the men it names.","Paul adds that most of the five hundred were still alive when he wrote, an open invitation to ask them. The Gospels then narrate appearances in Jerusalem and Galilee: to the women, to two disciples on the road to Emmaus, to the disciples behind locked doors, to Thomas, and by the lake."],"texts":[{"ref":"1 Corinthians 15:3–8","text":"For I delivered to you first of all that which I also received: that Christ died for our sins according to the Scriptures, that he was buried, that he was raised on the third day according to the Scriptures, and that he appeared to Cephas, then to the twelve. Then he appeared to over five hundred brothers at once, most of whom remain until now, but some have also fallen asleep. Then he appeared to James, then to all the apostles, and last of all, as to the child born at the wrong time, he appeared to me also.","doc":"scripture/new-testament/07-1-corinthians","at":"chapter-15"},{"ref":"Galatians 1:18–19","text":"Then after three years I went up to Jerusalem to visit Peter, and stayed with him fifteen days. But of the other apostles I saw no one except James, the Lord’s brother.","doc":"scripture/new-testament/09-galatians","at":"chapter-1"},{"ref":"John 20:19–20","text":"When therefore it was evening on that day, the first day of the week, and when the doors were locked where the disciples were assembled, for fear of the Jews, Jesus came and stood in the middle and said to them, “Peace be to you.” When he had said this, he showed them his hands and his side. The disciples therefore were glad when they saw the Lord.","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-20"}],"links":["scripture/new-testament/07-1-corinthians","scripture/new-testament/09-galatians","scripture/new-testament/03-luke","scripture/new-testament/04-john"],"art":"thomas"},{"id":"sceptics-who-turned","title":"The sceptics who turned","claim":"Two men who rejected Jesus during his life, his brother James and the persecutor Paul, became leaders of the movement after reporting that he had appeared to them.","body":["During the ministry Jesus’s own family did not follow him. Mark records that they tried to take him home, saying he was out of his mind, and John states plainly that his brothers did not believe in him. Yet soon after the crucifixion his brothers are among the believers in Jerusalem, and James becomes the leading figure of the church there.","The creed gives the reason: “then he appeared to James.” James held to it until his death. Josephus records that in AD 62 the high priest Ananus had “the brother of Jesus who was called Christ, whose name was James” brought before the council and stoned, a passage widely accepted as genuine.","Paul was a Pharisee, a strict teacher of the Jewish law, who set out to destroy the church. By his own account he changed course because the risen Jesus appeared to him. The churches of Judea, he says, heard only that their former persecutor was now preaching the faith he had tried to destroy."],"texts":[{"ref":"John 7:5","text":"For even his brothers didn’t believe in him.","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john","at":"chapter-7"},{"ref":"Josephus, Antiquities 20.200","text":"he assembled the sanhedrim of judges, and brought before them the brother of Jesus who was called Christ, whose name was James: and some others","doc":"witnesses/josephus-antiquities-20","at":"ant-20-9-1"},{"ref":"Galatians 1:13, 23","text":"beyond measure I persecuted the assembly of God and ravaged it. … “He who once persecuted us now preaches the faith that he once tried to destroy.”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/09-galatians","at":"chapter-1"}],"links":["witnesses/josephus-antiquities-20","scripture/new-testament/09-galatians","scripture/new-testament/11-philippians","archaeology/james-ossuary"],"art":"conversion-paul"},{"id":"earliest-proclamation","title":"The earliest proclamation","claim":"From the first weeks in Jerusalem the resurrection was the centre of the message, and within a generation Jesus was worshipped as Lord.","body":["Acts 2 sets the first public preaching at Pentecost, the Jewish harvest festival seven weeks after Passover, in Jerusalem. Its message is short: the man you crucified, God raised, and we are witnesses. The resurrection is not a later development of the Christian message; it is where the message begins.","Paul’s letters, written within twenty-five years of the crucifixion, already assume that Jesus is worshipped. He quotes an Aramaic prayer, “Maranatha”, meaning “Our Lord, come”, which must come from the Aramaic-speaking church of Judea, and in Philippians a hymn in which every knee bows at the name of Jesus.","Outside observers confirm the picture. Pliny the Younger, Roman governor of Bithynia in north-west Asia Minor, reported to the emperor Trajan c. AD 112 that Christians met before daybreak to sing a hymn to Christ as to a god; some he questioned had left the movement twenty years earlier."],"texts":[{"ref":"Acts 2:36","text":"“Let all the house of Israel therefore know certainly that God has made him both Lord and Christ, this Jesus whom you crucified.”","doc":"scripture/new-testament/05-acts","at":"chapter-2"},{"ref":"Philippians 2:9–11","text":"Therefore God also highly exalted him, and gave to him the name which is above every name, that at the name of Jesus every knee should bow, of those in heaven, those on earth, and those under the earth, and that every tongue should confess that Jesus Christ is Lord, to the glory of God the Father.","doc":"scripture/new-testament/11-philippians","at":"chapter-2"},{"ref":"Pliny, Letters 10.96","text":"on a stated day they had been accustomed to meet before daybreak and to recite a hymn among themselves to Christ, as though he were a god","doc":"legal/pliny-trajan-letters-10-96-97","at":"letter-10-96"}],"links":["scripture/new-testament/05-acts","scripture/new-testament/11-philippians","legal/pliny-trajan-letters-10-96-97","fathers/didache"],"art":"pentecost"},{"id":"witnesses-who-suffered","title":"Witnesses who suffered for it","claim":"The leading witnesses held to their testimony under persecution and death, and the generation after them insisted that Jesus rose in the body.","body":["Clement of Rome, writing for the church in Rome to the church at Corinth c. AD 96, recalls Peter and Paul as men of his own generation who were persecuted and put to death. Peter and Paul were not passing on a rumour; each claimed to have seen the risen Jesus himself. Men in that position knew whether what they proclaimed was true.","Ignatius, bishop of Antioch in Syria, wrote to the church at Smyrna c. AD 110 while being taken to Rome to die in the arena. Against teachers who said Christ only seemed to suffer, he insists that Jesus rose in the flesh, was touched by his disciples, and ate and drank with them.","Ignatius ties his own death to that conviction: if the resurrection happened only in appearance, then his chains are only an appearance too. He wrote those words on the road to his own execution. The first witnesses and the generation that knew them staked their lives on a bodily resurrection, and they did so openly, under their own names."],"texts":[{"ref":"1 Clement 5","text":"Peter, through unrighteous envy, endured not one or two, but numerous labours and when he had at length suffered martyrdom, departed to the place of glory due to him. Owing to envy, Paul also obtained the reward of patient endurance","doc":"fathers/1-clement","at":"ch-5"},{"ref":"Ignatius, To the Smyrnaeans 3","text":"For I know that after His resurrection also He was still possessed of flesh, and I believe that He is so now. When, for instance, He came to those who were with Peter, He said to them, \"Lay hold, handle Me, and see that I am not an incorporeal spirit.\" And immediately they touched Him, and believed","doc":"fathers/ignatius-smyrnaeans","at":"ch-3"},{"ref":"Ignatius, To the Smyrnaeans 4","text":"But if these things were done by our Lord only in appearance, then am I also only in appearance bound. And why have I also surrendered myself to death, to fire, to the sword, to the wild beasts?","doc":"fathers/ignatius-smyrnaeans","at":"ch-4"}],"links":["fathers/1-clement","fathers/ignatius-smyrnaeans","fathers/eusebius-he-3"],"art":"crucifixion-peter"}],"close":["Taken together, these lines converge: a public execution, a known tomb, a tomb found empty and conceded empty by opponents, women as first witnesses, a creed reaching back to the men who saw, a brother and a persecutor won over, worship of Jesus from the start, and witnesses who held firm to death. Any explanation has to account for all of it at once. The first Christians gave theirs in three words, “He has risen”, and the reader is invited to weigh whether any other account fits the evidence as well."]},"manuscripts":{"lede":"No original of any New Testament book survives, but thousands of copies do, some within decades or a few generations of the writing, more than for any other ancient book.","intro":["The New Testament books were written in Greek between c. AD 50 and c. AD 100, on papyrus that has long since perished. Like every ancient text, they reach us through copies of copies, made by hand until the printing press arrived in the fifteenth century.","What sets the New Testament apart is the number and age of those copies. Scraps of papyrus from the second and third centuries, complete Bibles from the fourth, early translations into other languages and thousands of quotations by early Christian writers allow scholars to trace the text back very close to its source."],"stats":[{"value":"5,800+","label":"Greek New Testament manuscripts catalogued, from small fragments to complete Bibles"},{"value":"140+","label":"papyrus manuscripts, most from Egypt and many from the 2nd–4th centuries"},{"value":"c. 10,000","label":"manuscripts of the Latin Vulgate translation"},{"value":"c. AD 350","label":"the earliest surviving complete New Testament, in Codex Sinaiticus"}],"witnesses":[{"id":"p52","name":"Papyrus 52 (Rylands)","date":"2nd century","contents":"John 18:31–33, 37–38","held":"John Rylands Library, Manchester","note":"A scrap the size of a credit card from a codex of John, found in Egypt. Proposed dates run from c. AD 100 to c. 200, and on any of them it is among the earliest surviving pieces of the New Testament.","art":"p52-john","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john"},{"id":"p46","name":"Papyrus 46 (Chester Beatty II)","date":"c. AD 200","contents":"Most of Paul’s letters and Hebrews","held":"Chester Beatty Library, Dublin, and University of Michigan, Ann Arbor","note":"The oldest surviving collection of Paul’s letters, copied as a single book about a century and a half after he wrote them. It already treats Paul’s letters as a set to be read together.","art":"p46-1cor","doc":"scripture/new-testament/07-1-corinthians"},{"id":"p45","name":"Papyrus 45 (Chester Beatty I)","date":"c. AD 250","contents":"Portions of all four Gospels and Acts","held":"Chester Beatty Library, Dublin (one leaf in Vienna)","note":"The earliest surviving manuscript to bind the four Gospels and Acts in one volume. Though damaged, it shows the fourfold Gospel circulating together in the third century.","art":"p45-mark","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark"},{"id":"p66","name":"Papyrus 66 (Bodmer II)","date":"c. AD 200 (some scholars argue later)","contents":"Most of John","held":"Fondation Martin Bodmer, Cologny, Geneva","note":"A nearly complete copy of John’s Gospel. Its scribe made hundreds of corrections in the course of the work, showing the text being checked with care as it was copied.","art":"p66-codex","doc":"scripture/new-testament/04-john"},{"id":"p75","name":"Papyrus 75 (Bodmer XIV–XV)","date":"c. AD 175–225 (some scholars argue later)","contents":"Much of Luke and John","held":"Vatican Library, Rome","note":"The earliest copy of Luke. Its text agrees remarkably closely with Codex Vaticanus, copied about a century later, showing a carefully preserved line of transmission.","art":"p75-luke","doc":"scripture/new-testament/03-luke"},{"id":"sinaiticus","name":"Codex Sinaiticus","date":"4th century (c. AD 330–360)","contents":"The complete New Testament, much of the Greek Old Testament, the Epistle of Barnabas and part of the Shepherd of Hermas","held":"British Library, London (with leaves in Leipzig, St Petersburg and St Catherine’s Monastery, Sinai)","note":"The earliest surviving complete New Testament, found at St Catherine’s Monastery at the foot of Mount Sinai in the nineteenth century. It is now fully digitised and readable online.","art":"sinaiticus-case","doc":null},{"id":"vaticanus","name":"Codex Vaticanus","date":"4th century (c. AD 300–350)","contents":"Most of the Greek Bible; the New Testament breaks off at Hebrews 9:14 and lacks the Pastoral Letters, Philemon and Revelation","held":"Vatican Library, Rome","note":"Perhaps the most valued single manuscript for the Gospels. With Sinaiticus it is one of the two great fourth-century Bibles on which modern editions rest.","art":"vaticanus-matthew","doc":null},{"id":"alexandrinus","name":"Codex Alexandrinus","date":"5th century","contents":"Nearly the whole Greek Bible, with some losses in the New Testament, followed by 1 and 2 Clement","held":"British Library, London","note":"Long kept by the patriarchs of Alexandria and given to King Charles I of England in 1627. It also preserves the letter of Clement of Rome to the Corinthians.","art":"alex-mark","doc":"fathers/1-clement"},{"id":"bezae","name":"Codex Bezae","date":"c. AD 400 (late 4th or 5th century)","contents":"The Gospels and Acts in Greek and Latin on facing pages, with a fragment of 3 John","held":"Cambridge University Library","note":"The chief witness to the so-called Western text, which differs most from other manuscripts in Acts. It shows the kind of freer copying scholars weigh when they compare witnesses.","art":"bezae-john","doc":"scripture/new-testament/05-acts"},{"id":"washingtonianus","name":"Codex Washingtonianus (W)","date":"late 4th or 5th century","contents":"The four Gospels, in the order Matthew, John, Luke, Mark","held":"Freer Gallery of Art, Smithsonian Institution, Washington, DC","note":"Bought in Egypt in 1906. Its copy of Mark includes the longer ending with an extra saying after 16:14 found in no other Greek manuscript, a vivid example of how variant endings of Mark circulated.","art":"w-mark16","doc":"scripture/new-testament/02-mark"}],"compare":[{"work":"New Testament","author":"Various","written":"c. AD 50–100","earliest":"2nd century (fragments); c. AD 200–250 (substantial papyri); 4th century (complete)","gap":"Decades for the earliest fragments; c. 250–300 years for a complete copy","copies":"5,800+ Greek, many fragmentary; c. 10,000 Latin"},{"work":"Annals","author":"Tacitus","written":"c. AD 116","earliest":"9th century (books 1–6); 11th century (books 11–16)","gap":"c. 750–950 years","copies":"2 primary manuscripts"},{"work":"Gallic War","author":"Julius Caesar","written":"c. 58–50 BC","earliest":"9th century","gap":"c. 900 years","copies":"c. 250"},{"work":"Lives of the Caesars","author":"Suetonius","written":"c. AD 121","earliest":"9th century","gap":"c. 700 years","copies":"c. 200"},{"work":"Jewish Antiquities","author":"Josephus","written":"c. AD 93–94","earliest":"9th century (Latin translation); 11th century (Greek, books 11–20)","gap":"c. 800–1,000 years","copies":"A few Greek manuscripts for each part; more in Latin"},{"work":"Iliad","author":"Homer","written":"c. 8th century BC","earliest":"3rd century BC (papyrus fragments); 10th century AD (complete)","gap":"c. 400–500 years to the earliest fragments","copies":"Well over 1,000, most of them papyrus fragments"},{"work":"Dialogues","author":"Plato","written":"c. 390–347 BC","earliest":"3rd century BC (papyrus fragments); AD 895 (complete codex)","gap":"c. 1,250 years to the earliest complete codex","copies":"c. 250 medieval manuscripts, plus papyrus fragments"}],"points":[{"title":"What the numbers show","body":"No other ancient book comes close to the New Testament in the number of its manuscripts or their nearness to the time of writing. For most classical authors, scholars rely on a handful of medieval copies; for the New Testament they have **thousands**, some from the second and third centuries. Many are fragments or lectionaries (books of readings for worship), but **the earliest papyri alone give a fuller and older record than survives for Tacitus or Caesar.**"},{"title":"Lives compared","body":"Alexander the Great died in 323 BC; the earliest surviving account of his campaigns, by Diodorus of Sicily, came **nearly three centuries later**, and the fullest, by Plutarch and Arrian, more than four. The Buddha’s teaching was passed on by memory for centuries before the Pali Canon was written down around the first century BC. For Jesus, Paul’s letters date within **about twenty-five years of the crucifixion**, the Gospels within forty to seventy, and Josephus, Tacitus, Pliny and Suetonius mention him or his followers within a century. **No historian doubts that Alexander lived; the reader can weigh what an earlier and fuller record means for Jesus.**"},{"title":"How textual criticism works","body":"Textual criticism recovers the original wording of a text from its surviving copies. Scholars compare the manuscripts reading by reading, group them into families, give weight to the oldest and most careful, and ask which reading best explains the others. **Because the New Testament witnesses are so many and so widespread, a mistake made in one place is usually exposed by copies from elsewhere.** Modern critical editions print the result and list the main alternatives at the foot of each page."},{"title":"What the variants are like","body":"Hand copying produces differences, and across thousands of manuscripts they add up. **Most are minor**: spelling, word order, a dropped or repeated word, a synonym. A smaller number change the sense of a sentence. The few significant ones, such as the longer ending of Mark (16:9–20) and the woman caught in adultery (John 7:53–8:11), are well known and marked in modern Bibles with a note or brackets, **in plain view for any reader to check.**"},{"title":"Early translations","body":"As the faith spread, the New Testament was translated: into Latin in the second and third centuries, before Jerome’s Vulgate (c. AD 382–405), and into Syriac and Coptic for the churches of Syria, Mesopotamia and Egypt from the third century. The translators worked from Greek manuscripts older than most that survive, so their versions show what the Greek read in their region and time."},{"title":"The Fathers’ quotations","body":"Early Christian writers quoted the New Testament constantly. Irenaeus (c. AD 180), Tertullian, **Clement of Alexandria and Origen cite it so often that much of the text could be rebuilt from their works alone**. Their quotations show which readings were known where and when, and confirm that the books were being read and copied across the Mediterranean **well before the great fourth-century Bibles**."}]}});
